CSRF:
====
CSRF stands for cross site resource forgery. This is a security vulnerability in your web application where the third party user or application will trick the user
to click on the link or website and submit the form in background in the application in which the user is already authenticated.

This is called CSRF. 

Same-Site Cookies: Browsers now support the "SameSite" attribute for cookies, which restricts cookies to only be sent with requests from the same 
origin as the one that set the cookie.

It’s a security measure used to prevent CSRF attacks.
A CSRF token is a unique, unpredictable value generated by the server and included in forms or requests.
When the server receives a request, it checks if the token matches the one it issued. If not, the request is rejected.
This ensures that the request came from the legitimate site and not from a malicious third-party.

