Basic Flow of sprinng security:
----------------------------------
1. User submits credentials

The user sends a POST request to /login (for form login) or uses HTTP Basic/JWT.
Credentials: username + password.


2. Request hits Spring Security Filter Chain

Spring Security registers a DelegatingFilterProxy in the servlet container.
This delegates to SpringSecurityFilterChain, which contains multiple filters:

UsernamePasswordAuthenticationFilter (for form login)
BasicAuthenticationFilter (for HTTP Basic)
CsrfFilter, etc.


3. UsernamePasswordAuthenticationFilter triggers

Extracts username and password from the request.
Creates an Authentication object (specifically UsernamePasswordAuthenticationToken) with these credentials (not yet authenticated).


4. AuthenticationManager kicks in

The filter calls AuthenticationManager.authenticate(authentication).
By default, Spring uses ProviderManager with one or more AuthenticationProviders.
For username/password, the default provider is DaoAuthenticationProvider.


5. DaoAuthenticationProvider logic

Calls your configured UserDetailsService.loadUserByUsername(username).

If you didn’t configure one, Spring uses InMemoryUserDetailsManager by default.


UserDetailsService returns a UserDetails object with:

Encoded password
Roles/authorities
Account status flags




6. Password verification

DaoAuthenticationProvider uses the configured PasswordEncoder (e.g., BCryptPasswordEncoder) to compare:

Raw password from the request
Encoded password from UserDetails




7. Authentication success

If passwords match and account is valid:

Creates a fully authenticated Authentication object.
Stores it in SecurityContextHolder.


If failure:

Throws BadCredentialsException or UsernameNotFoundException.




8. SecurityContext persistence

SecurityContextPersistenceFilter saves the SecurityContext in the HTTP session (for stateful apps).
For stateless APIs (JWT), you skip sessions and rely on tokens.


9. Response

If successful:

Redirects to the default page or the requested resource.


If failure:

Redirects to /login?error or returns 401 Unauthorized for APIs.]]


Delegation Proxy Filter:
========================

DelegatingFilterProxy is a Spring-provided Servlet Filter that acts as a bridge between the Servlet container and Spring’s application context.

✅ What is it?

It’s a standard javax.servlet.Filter implementation.
Instead of implementing security logic itself, it delegates all work to a Spring-managed bean (usually the springSecurityFilterChain).
This allows Spring Security filters to partici7pate in the Servlet filter chain without being registered manually in web.xml or programmatically.


✅ Why is it useful?

Integration point: Servlet containers (Tomcat, Jetty) know about filters, not Spring beans. DelegatingFilterProxy connects them.
Decoupling: You configure your security filters in Spring, not in the container.
Flexibility: The actual filter logic lives in Spring beans, so you can use dependency injection and Spring configuration.


✅ How it works internally

The container sees DelegatingFilterProxy registered as a filter.
When a request comes in, DelegatingFilterProxy looks up a bean in the Spring context with the same name (by default springSecurityFilterChain).
It delegates doFilter() calls to that bean.
That bean is actually a FilterChainProxy that runs all Spring Security filters (CSRF, authentication, etc.).


✅ In Spring Boot

You don’t need to register it manually; Boot auto-registers DelegatingFilterProxy for springSecurityFilterChain.
This is why Spring Security works out-of-the-box without touching web.xml.


✅ Example (traditional XML)
XML<filter>    <filter-name>springSecurityFilterChain</filter-name>    <filter-class>org.springframework.web.filter.DelegatingFilterProxy</filter-class></filter><filter-mapping>    <filter-name>springSecurityFilterChain</filter-name>    <url-pattern>/*</url-pattern></filter-mapping>Show more lines
In modern Spring Boot, this is done automatically.

✅ Summary

DelegatingFilterProxy = a bridge filter that delegates to a Spring bean.
Why needed? Servlet containers manage filters, Spring manages beans. This connects them.
Used for: Spring Security filter chain, but can also delegate to any custom filter bean.


