Authemtication provider and SecurityContext:
------------------------------------------

Authentication provider is an interface which is responsible for authenticating the incoming request by delegating the request to the appropriate authentication provider.

Authentication provider will make use of Provider Manager which iterates through all the available authentication providers and chosses the appropriate provider.

Post Completion of the Authentication, if the authentication is succesful it returns fully authenticated object and this authenticated object is stored in SecurityContext.

Storing the authenticated object in the securitycontext allows any part of the application to easily access the current user info without passing the info manually.

When you use annotations like @PreAuthorize("hasRole('ADMIN')") or hasAuthority(), Spring Security checks the Authentication object from the SecurityContext.
Without storing it, these checks wouldn’t work.

For stateful apps (e.g., form login), the SecurityContext can be persisted in the HTTP session.
This allows the user to remain authenticated across multiple requests without re-login.


SecurityContextHolder:
----------------------
SecurityContextHolder itself is not a filter, but it works closely with Spring Security’s filter chain to store and retrieve the SecurityContext during request processing.

It provides static access to the SecurityContext for the current thread.
The SecurityContext contains the Authentication object (user details, roles, etc.).
By default, it uses ThreadLocal to store this context per request thread.
